      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sterling’s name comes from the historic Norman silver coin, the sterling, echoing a 12th‑century monetary tradition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sterling%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Old Sterling, once called Guilford and later Loudoun, once vied with nearby Ashburn to lure tourists and businesses.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.loudounhistory.org/history/sterling-guilford-early-history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Railroad tracks once marked the southern edge of what is today Sterling Park, shaping the town’s early layout.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
